    "q-215127": {
      "dom_id": "step-q215127",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Written in **ascending order**, the solutions to $|5y-1|=6$ are $y=${{field-1}} and $y=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|5y-1|=5y-1,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5y-1| & =6 \\\\ 5y-1 & =6 \\\\ 5y & =7 \\\\ \\frac{5y}{5} & =\\frac{7}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|5y-1|=-(5y-1),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5y-1| & =6 \\\\ -(5y-1) & =6 \\\\ -5y+1 & =6 \\\\ -5y & =5 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & =\\frac{5}{-5} \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
